pluginlib allows you to load classes at runtime without knowing their concrete type at compile time — the same pattern Nav2 uses for planners, controllers, and costmap layers.

1. Define the base class (in a header, C++):
   class BasePlugin {
   public:
     virtual void initialize(rclcpp::Node::SharedPtr node) = 0;
     virtual void execute() = 0;
     virtual ~BasePlugin() {}
   };

2. Implement the plugin:
   class MyPlugin : public my_pkg::BasePlugin {
   public:
     void initialize(...) override { ... }
     void execute() override { ... }
   };
   // Register:
   #include <pluginlib/class_list_macros.hpp>
   PLUGINLIB_EXPORT_CLASS(my_pkg::MyPlugin, my_pkg::BasePlugin)

3. Declare in plugin.xml:
   <library path="my_plugin_lib">
     <class name="my_pkg/MyPlugin" type="my_pkg::MyPlugin" base_class_type="my_pkg::BasePlugin">
       <description>My plugin</description>
     </class>
   </library>

4. Register in package.xml:
   <export><plugin plugin="${prefix}/plugin.xml"/></export>

5. Load at runtime:
   pluginlib::ClassLoader<BasePlugin> loader("my_pkg", "my_pkg::BasePlugin");
   auto plugin = loader.createSharedInstance("my_pkg/MyPlugin");

Python pluginlib exists but is less common — most plugin-heavy packages (Nav2, ros2_control) use C++.

A hardware interface plugin bridges ros2_control's controller layer to the physical actuators. It must implement three methods: on_init(), read(), and write().

Minimal skeleton:
  #include <hardware_interface/system_interface.hpp>
  class MyMotorHW : public hardware_interface::SystemInterface {
  public:
    hardware_interface::CallbackReturn on_init(const hardware_interface::HardwareInfo& info) override {
      // Read URDF parameters (port, baud rate, etc.)
      // Open serial/CAN connection
      return hardware_interface::CallbackReturn::SUCCESS;
    }
    hardware_interface::return_type read(const rclcpp::Time&, const rclcpp::Duration&) override {
      // Read encoder counts from hardware
      // Write to state_interfaces_ (position_, velocity_)
    }
    hardware_interface::return_type write(const rclcpp::Time&, const rclcpp::Duration&) override {
      // Read from command_interfaces_ (effort_ or velocity_)
      // Send to hardware (serial command, CAN frame, etc.)
    }
  };
  PLUGINLIB_EXPORT_CLASS(my_pkg::MyMotorHW, hardware_interface::SystemInterface)

The controller_manager calls read() then all controllers then write() in a tight loop at the configured control rate (typically 100-500 Hz for motor control).

In the URDF, declare the hardware plugin and its joints under <ros2_control> tags. The joint names in the URDF must match what your hardware interface exports as state/command interfaces.
